## Summary

`serializeIONode` picks the name of an I/O entry from the name of the first first-party function in the stack. That name can include the type name that the function was called on. Since that context is usually not useful, the name is reduced to just the function, e.g. `Window.fetch` becomes `fetch`.

The `<anonymous>.` case copied the slice length straight from the `Window.` case above it, but that prefix is **12 characters long, not 7**. So instead of stripping the prefix it damaged the name:

```
'<anonymous>.'.length   === 12
'<anonymous>.fetch'.slice(7)  === 'ous>.fetch'   // current, broken
'<anonymous>.fetch'.slice(12) === 'fetch'        // intended
```

This was introduced in #33392 and is the only place in the repo that strips this prefix.

### Reproducing

V8 derives the type name of a method call from the receiver, and `Symbol.toStringTag` is honored there. An object that reports itself as `<anonymous>` therefore produces stack frames named `<anonymous>.getData`:

```js
const client = {
  [Symbol.toStringTag]: '<anonymous>',
  getData(text) {
    return new Promise(resolve => {
      setTimeout(() => resolve(text.toUpperCase()), 1);
    });
  },
};
```

`CallSite.prototype.toString()` for that frame is `<anonymous>.getData (file:1:1)`, so this shape is produced by both the structured stack parser and the string stack parser.

